<% content_for :title, 'Edit planetoid belt' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Edit Planetoid Belt' do %>
  <div class='space-y-8'>
    <p class='text-fg'>
      Manual changes are not validated against the rules in the World Builder's Handbook (WBH). Any value entered here is
      stored as given, so check it against the WBH yourself.
    </p>

    <p class='text-fg'>
      The form has one tab for each part of the belt's UWP: General, Orbital, Starport, Physical, Atmosphere,
      Hydrographics, Population, Government, Law Level and Tech Level. It is a single form, so values on every tab are
      saved together.
    </p>

    <p class='text-fg'>
      Fields marked with the chip icon are required when adding a planetoid belt: orbit, period, inclination,
      eccentricity, every UWP character (starport, atmosphere, hydrographics, population, government, law level and tech level), the four composition percentages, resource rating, bulk and span. Enter 0 for any composition
      percentage that does not apply.
    </p>

    <div>
      <%= render 'application/subsection', title: 'Orbital tab' %>
      <dl class='mt-4 space-y-5'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Orbit</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Orbit number around the parent star, no lower than the star's minimum allowable orbit.
            The AU distance and orbit sequence are derived from it.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Period</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Orbital period in days.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Retrograde</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Whether the belt orbits against the direction of the star's rotation.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Inclination and Eccentricity</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Inclination in degrees; eccentricity from 0 (circular) upwards.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <%= render 'application/subsection', title: 'Physical tab' %>
      <dl class='mt-4 space-y-5'>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Metallic, Stony, Carbonaceous and Other</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Percentage of each material type. When any is non-zero the four must total 100%.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Resource rating</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Resource rating from 2 to 12.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Bulk</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Bulk of the belt, a whole number of at least 1.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Span</dt>
          <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>Width of the belt in orbit numbers.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <%= render 'application/subsection', title: 'Other tabs' %>
      <p class='mt-4 text-fg'>
        The Starport, Atmosphere, Hydrographics, Population, Government, Law Level and Tech Level tabs each hold a
        required UWP character and follow the same rules as other worlds. See the help for editing a world for details. The Physical tab also holds
        the mean temperature in kelvin.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
